MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two defects the first live runs hit, both mine, both reproduced here before fixing.

**"600s with zero log output."** Piped through PowerShell or a tee, Python block-buffers stdout, so a
slow-but-working turn printed nothing until it exited — indistinguishable from a hang. The script now
reconfigures stdout/stderr to line buffering *at import* (the child engine already got
PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1; this is the same courtesy for the parent, and at import it covers early failures
too). Verified: with stdout on a pipe, the first line arrives in 0.03s while the process is still
running. The heartbeat also says something useful now — elapsed time, frames received, and the last
frame type — every 30s instead of a bare "still waiting" every 120s, so a quiet provider can be told
apart from a dead one.

**"`--diff` still prints its vacuous 'no changes'."** It shelled out to `git diff --stat`, which prints
nothing and exits 128 outside a repository; only the *exception* was caught, never the exit code, so
`--demo` (a temp copy, never a repo) reported no changes while the agent had in fact edited the CSS.
A diff that lies is worse than no diff. The evidence now comes from the filesystem: a before/after
snapshot of content, reported as `M styles.css +6 -0` with a short unified diff per file, `A`/`D` for
added and removed files, and an honest `no file changed (N file(s) compared)` when nothing moved.
Dependency noise (.git, node_modules, .venv, __pycache__ …) is skipped and files over 4 MB are
compared by size. `git diff --stat` still prints as *extra* context when the workspace really is a
repository — and now checks the exit code before believing it.

Also: the demo batch's first selector was not valid CSS (`header nav-actions`, where nav-actions is a
class), and `run_turn` returns a tuple now, so its docstring says so.

Ten pins in src/tests/test_preview_batch_turn_script.py. Three matter: line buffering is asserted in a
child process with a piped stdout (in-process pytest would not answer the question), verified to fail
when the import-time call is removed; the diff is driven through modify/add/remove and
`git_diff_stat` is asserted to return None outside a repository (the vacuous report reproduced exactly
as "git reported no changes"); and every fingerprint in the demo batch is asserted to still exist in
the lab fixture — drift there is invisible and would look like a prompt-engineering failure instead of
a stale fixture.

The runbook gains §7.5: what the harness is, the two commands, what a pass looks like (the agent
searched for the fingerprint classes, edited only the stylesheet, verified), the two gotchas above, and
what it does not cover — the fixture is static HTML/CSS, so component libraries, Tailwind and
CSS-in-JS are untested against a real model, which is exactly where fingerprints matter most.

83 passed. No TypeScript in this change, so Gate A is untouched by it.

